        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "English had an older expression, make or mar, which communicated a similar contrast between success and ruin. Historical proverb records show that make or mar was used centuries before the modern wording. However, the exact process through which break replaced mar has not been firmly established, so the expression should not be connected to one dramatic invention story.",
            "zh": "英語中較早已有 make or mar 這個表達，同樣用來對比成功與毀壞。 歷史諺語記錄顯示，make or mar 在現代說法出現前數百年已有人使用。 不過，break 如何取代 mar 的確切過程仍未能肯定， 因此不應把這個表達說成源自某一個戲劇性的事件。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "The strong contrast naturally came to describe a person, action or decision capable of causing either success or failure. Merriam-Webster records the related hyphenated adjective make-or-break from 1919, although that date should not be treated as the invention date of every related use.",
            "zh": "這種強烈對比逐漸用來形容足以令事情成功或失敗的人物、行動或決定。 Merriam-Webster 記錄了帶連字號的形容詞 make-or-break 在1919年的用法，但不應把該年份視為所有相關用法的創造年份。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "One important factor may build success—or destroy the chance of it.",
        "memoryZh": "一項重要因素可以成就成功，也可以摧毀成功的機會。"
