      "origin": {
        "statusEn": "Exact Short-Form Origin Unknown; Related Longer Form Documented",
        "statusZh": "確切簡短形式來源不明；相關較長形式已有記錄",
        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "Psalm 66:12 in English Bible translations contains the image of people who “went through fire and water” before reaching safety. This demonstrates an old religious and literary image of surviving severe trials, but it does not prove when the exact modern expression was invented. Major learner dictionaries document the longer form go through fire and water rather than establishing a first use of go through fire alone. Cambridge describes the longer expression as UK old-fashioned.",
            "zh": "英文《聖經》詩篇第66篇第12節包含人們經過「火與水」後到達安穩之地的畫面。 這顯示以火與水象徵嚴峻考驗的意象由來已久，但不能證明確切的現代表達是在何時創造。 主要學習字典收錄的是較完整的 go through fire and water，而未能確認 go through fire 這個簡短形式的首次使用時間。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "The physical image of surviving fire came to represent enduring pain, danger and sacrifice. The shorter go through fire retains the strongest part of that image and is understood as a dramatic metaphor.",
            "zh": "捱過真正火焰的畫面逐漸用來代表承受痛苦、危險和犧牲。 簡短形式 go through fire 保留了這個意象中最強烈的部分，成為富有戲劇性的比喻。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "Surviving fire requires exceptional strength; going through fire for a goal means refusing to give up despite exceptional hardship.",
        "memoryZh": "捱過火焰需要非凡力量；為目標 go through fire，便表示即使面對極大艱辛也不放棄。"
      },
