      "benefits": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "titleEn": "It Gives a Gentle Description of Mild Illness",
          "titleZh": "委婉地描述輕微不適",
          "en": "The idiomatic version sounds less clinical and does not require the speaker to name a specific illness.",
          "zh": "慣用語版本的語氣較輕鬆，也不需要說話者指出確切疾病。",
          "examples": []
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "titleEn": "It Helps Explain a Temporary Change of Plan",
          "titleZh": "解釋暫時更改計劃的原因",
          "en": "The expression gives a clear but brief reason for cancelling or changing a plan.",
          "zh": "我有點不舒服，所以今晚會留在家中。 這個表達可以簡短而清楚地說明取消或更改計劃的原因。",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "I’m feeling under the weather, so I’ll stay home tonight.",
              "zh": "我有點不舒服，所以今晚會留在家中。",
              "highlight": "under the weather"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "titleEn": "It Avoids Unnecessary Medical Details",
          "titleZh": "避免交代不必要的醫療細節",
          "en": "A person may not want to describe every symptom.",
          "zh": "說話者可能不想詳細說明所有症狀。 我自昨天開始一直有點不舒服。",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "I’ve been feeling under the weather since yesterday.",
              "zh": "我自昨天開始一直有點不舒服。",
              "highlight": "under the weather"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 4,
          "titleEn": "It Is Useful for Friendly Questions",
          "titleZh": "適合用來關心別人",
          "en": "This can sound caring when it is said gently and in an appropriate relationship.",
          "zh": "你看起來有點不舒服。 你還好嗎？ 只要語氣溫和，而且雙方關係合適，這個說法可以自然地表達關心。",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "You look a little under the weather. Are you all right?",
              "zh": "你看起來有點不舒服。你還好嗎？",
              "highlight": "under the weather"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 5,
          "titleEn": "It Shows That the Condition Is Probably Temporary",
          "titleZh": "表示情況可能只是暫時的",
          "en": "The expression often suggests that the person expects to recover after some rest.",
          "zh": "我昨天有點不舒服，但現在已經沒事。 這個表達通常暗示說話者休息後便可能康復。",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "I was under the weather yesterday, but I’m fine now.",
              "zh": "我昨天有點不舒服，但現在已經沒事。",
              "highlight": "under the weather"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 6,
          "titleEn": "It Is an Alternative, Not an Automatically Better Expression",
          "titleZh": "是一種替代說法，而非必然更好的說法",
          "en": "Under the weather is useful when the situation is mild and informal. In serious or formal situations, a plain and precise expression may be safer.",
          "zh": "當情況輕微而且語境較輕鬆時，under the weather 十分實用。 在嚴重或正式情況中，直接而準確的說法可能更合適。",
          "examples": []
        }
      ],
      "origin": {
        "statusEn": "Exact Origin Unknown; Probable Nautical Development",
        "statusZh": "確切來源不明；很可能由航海用語演變而來",
        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "The exact first use has not been identified. Historical evidence records under the weather in the sense of “indisposed” by about 1810, with an earlier use meaning “in difficulties” in 1803. The expression was colloquial and was apparently associated with sailors. Older nautical language also used under the weather shore for a protected place where the water was calmer.",
            "zh": "這個表達確切的首次用法尚未能確認。 歷史資料顯示，under the weather 約於 1810 年已用來表示「身體不適」，而 1803 年亦有一個表示「身處困境」的較早期用法。 這個短語當時屬於口語，並似乎與水手用語有關。 較早期的航海語言亦會用 under the weather shore 表示水面較平靜的避風位置。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "One popular explanation says that sick or seasick sailors went below deck to rest away from rough weather. Other historical accounts connect the expression with sheltered water or protected anchorage. Because the available accounts differ, the exact story should not be treated as proven. The general nautical image probably developed into the idea of temporarily withdrawing and recovering until conditions improved.",
            "zh": "一個流行說法指出，生病或暈船的水手會走到甲板下休息，避開惡劣天氣。 另一些歷史解釋則把這個表達與避風水域或安全停泊位置聯繫起來。 由於不同資料的說法並不完全相同，因此不應把任何一個故事視為已證實的確切來源。 這個航海畫面很可能逐漸演變成暫時退下休息，等待情況改善的意思。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "Imagine somebody temporarily sheltering from a storm: the person is not at their best but expects the difficult period to pass.",
        "memoryZh": "想像一個人暫時躲避風雨：他的狀態不太好，但預期這段不適很快便會過去。"
      },
