            "en": "The exact first use has not been identified. One early record located for this worksheet is an 1890 American newspaper heading, “Meeting Trouble Half Way”. A 1911 newspaper later printed the advice “Do not meet trouble half-way” beside the contrasting saying “Prepare for a rainy day”. An Irish folklore collection also recorded “Never meet trouble half-way” as an old saying. These records show that the figurative idea was established by the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, but they do not identify its inventor or exact starting point. Today, the expression advises people not to create unnecessary anxiety by assuming that a possible problem has already become real.",
