      "benefits": [
        {
          "number": 1,
          "titleEn": "It Describes Persistent Irritation",
          "titleZh": "表達持續而難以忽視的煩躁",
          "en": "The idiom is useful when something does not merely annoy somebody once, but continues to occupy their attention.",
          "zh": "這個慣用語適合描述某件事不只是惹人煩躁一次，而是持續令人介意。",
          "examples": []
        },
        {
          "number": 2,
          "titleEn": "It Expresses a Personal Emotional Reaction",
          "titleZh": "表達個人的情緒反應",
          "en": "Different people may be annoyed by different things. The possessive form clearly identifies whose feelings are affected. That noise gets under my skin, but it does not bother my sister.",
          "zh": "不同的人可能會被不同事情惹惱。 所有格可以清楚指出是誰受到情緒影響。 那種噪音令我十分心煩，但我的妹妹並不介意。",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "That noise gets under my skin, but it does not bother my sister.",
              "zh": "那種噪音令我十分心煩，但我的妹妹並不介意。",
              "highlight": "gets under my skin"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 3,
          "titleEn": "It Can Describe Deliberate Provocation",
          "titleZh": "描述故意挑釁",
          "en": "The expression can show that somebody is intentionally saying or doing something to produce an emotional reaction. He is only trying to get under your skin.",
          "zh": "這個表達可以指出某人故意說話或做事，以引起另一個人的情緒反應。 他只是想故意惹惱你。",
          "examples": [
            {
              "en": "He is only trying to get under your skin.",
              "zh": "他只是想故意惹惱你。",
              "highlight": "get under your skin"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "number": 4,
          "titleEn": "It Is Useful for Advice About Self-Control",
          "titleZh": "適合用來勸人保持冷靜",
          "en": "Do not let it get under your skin is a natural way to advise somebody not to give an irritating person the reaction they want.",
          "zh": "Do not let it get under your skin 是一種自然的勸告方式，提醒別人不要因惱人的言行而失去冷靜。",
          "examples": []
        },
        {
          "number": 5,
          "titleEn": "It Provides a Concise Alternative",
          "titleZh": "把較長的解釋縮短",
          "en": "His repeated comments are getting under my skin.",
          "zh": "這個慣用語把「持續惹惱」和「難以忽視」兩層意思集中在一個表達之中。",
          "examples": []
        },
        {
          "number": 6,
          "titleEn": "It Is Not Automatically Better Than “Annoy”",
          "titleZh": "並非在所有情況下都比 annoy 更好",
          "en": "Use the idiom when the personal and continuing nature of the irritation matters. In formal or emotionally neutral writing, annoy or cause frustration may be clearer.",
          "zh": "當持續而個人化的煩躁感十分重要時，可以使用這個慣用語。 在正式或客觀的文章中，annoy 或 cause frustration 可能更清楚。",
          "examples": []
        }
      ],
      "origin": {
        "statusEn": "Exact Origin Unknown; Gradual Metaphorical Development",
        "statusZh": "確切來源不明；逐漸形成比喻意思",
        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "The exact first idiomatic use has not been firmly identified. However, the negative figurative meaning was clearly established by the early twentieth century. Mary Roberts Rinehart’s 1920 novel A Poor Wise Man includes a character saying that a political idea “gets under my skin”, meaning that it strongly irritates him.",
            "zh": "這個慣用語的確切首次用法尚未能確認。 不過，到了二十世紀初，以它表示煩躁的比喻意思已經相當明確。 Mary Roberts Rinehart 在1920年的小說《A Poor Wise Man》中，便讓一名角色說某個政治觀念「gets under my skin」，意思是那個觀念令他十分惱火。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "A physical irritation beneath the skin is difficult to escape or ignore. Speakers gradually applied the same image to words, habits and people that remain in the mind and continue causing annoyance. This is the most plausible general explanation, although no single moment of creation has been proved.",
            "zh": "皮膚下的刺激令人難以擺脫或忽視。 後來，說話者逐漸把相同畫面用於某些說話、習慣或人物：它們會一直留在腦海中，持續令人煩躁。 這是最合理的一般解釋，但目前沒有證據證明這個表達在某一個確切時刻被創造。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "Something irritating under the skin is hard to ignore; an irritating person or habit can feel equally difficult to ignore.",
        "memoryZh": "皮膚下的刺激難以忽視；一個惱人的人或習慣也可能同樣令人無法不去介意。"
      },
