        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "A disapproving figurative use of black sheep is documented as early as 1640. Oxford connects the later established expression with the proverb “There is a black sheep in every flock,” which was in use by the late eighteenth century.",
            "zh": "早至1640年的文獻，已出現帶有貶義的 black sheep 比喻用法。 Oxford 把後來較固定的表達與 「每個羊群裡都有一隻黑羊」這句諺語連繫起來； 這句諺語在十八世紀末已有人使用。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "The unusual sheep gradually became a metaphor for a person whom a family or group regarded as disreputable or embarrassing. In modern English, the label may describe genuine misconduct, but it can also reveal unfair group pressure against a person who is simply different.",
            "zh": "這隻與其他羊不同的羊，逐漸成為一個比喻，指家族或群體中被視為名聲不佳或令人難堪的人。 現代英語中的這個標籤既可以指真正的失當行為，也可以反映群體只因某人與眾不同而施加的不公平壓力。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "One unusual sheep stands out in the flock; one disapproved member stands out in the group.",
        "memoryZh": "一隻與眾不同的羊在羊群中格外顯眼；一名不獲認同的成員也會在群體中被挑出來。"
