        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "Merriam-Webster dates its first known figurative use to 1910 and explains the phrase as coming from the veneration of cows by Hindus. Collins gives a similar dating of approximately 1905–1910 and also connects the expression with the traditional protected status of cattle in Hinduism. These dates refer to early documented English uses; they do not prove that one identifiable person invented the expression at that exact moment.",
            "zh": "Merriam-Webster 把目前已知的早期比喻用法定於1910年，並指出這個短語與印度教徒對牛的尊崇有關。 Collins 提供相近的1905至1910年時期，並同樣把這個表達與印度教傳統中牛受到保護的地位連繫起來。 這些年份只代表目前找到的早期英語書面記錄，並不能證明某一個人在該年確切創造了這個表達。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "English speakers transferred the image of a specially protected animal to people, beliefs, customs and institutions. A person or idea that nobody dared to attack, question or change could therefore be described figuratively as a sacred cow.",
            "zh": "英語使用者把受到特殊保護的動物這個意象，引申到人物、信念、習俗和機構之上。 因此，若沒有人敢批評、質疑或改變某個人物或觀念，便可以把它比喻為 a sacred cow。"
          }
        ],
