      "origin": {
        "statusEn": "Gradual Metaphorical Development; Exact First Use Uncertain",
        "statusZh": "逐漸形成的比喻用法；確切首次用法不明",
        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "Related wording involving licking oneself clean or whole has been traced by reference works to the sixteenth century, although the precise first use of the modern expression is uncertain. John Dryden’s 1677 play All for Love contains an early figurative example in which defeated forces withdraw and lick their wounds. This shows that the image was already being used metaphorically in the seventeenth century, but it does not prove who first created the expression.",
            "zh": "有參考資料把較早期的相關說法追溯至十六世紀，但現代形式的確切首次用法仍未能確認。 John Dryden 在1677年的劇作《All for Love》中，已使用「敗方退下來舔舐傷口」的比喻畫面。 這證明相關比喻在十七世紀已經出現，但不能證明是誰最先創造這個表達。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "A wounded animal temporarily withdraws and attends to its injuries. Speakers extended this physical image to people, teams and organisations recovering emotionally or practically after defeat, embarrassment, rejection or failure.",
            "zh": "受傷動物會暫時退下來處理傷處。 後來，人們把這個身體畫面延伸至個人、球隊或組織在落敗、 尷尬、被拒或失敗後休整。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "The animal withdraws to recover from an injury; the person withdraws to recover from a setback.",
        "memoryZh": "動物退下來養傷；人則退下來平復挫敗。"
      },
