      "origin": {
        "statusEn": "Advertising Origin Well Documented; Exact First Coinage Uncertain",
        "statusZh": "廣告來源有充分記錄；確切首次創作仍未完全確定",
        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "The expression is closely connected with twentieth-century petrol advertising. ExxonMobil’s official history records that an advertising copywriter in Chicago produced the slogan in 1959. Historical newspaper research has also found a similar “tiger power” image in 1952 and exact or closely related versions used by petrol companies in 1959 and 1960. The safe conclusion is that the advertising origin is clear, although identifying one unquestionable first creator is more difficult.",
            "zh": "這個表達與二十世紀的汽油廣告有密切關係。 ExxonMobil 的官方歷史記錄指出，一名芝加哥廣告文案撰稿人在1959年創作了相關口號。 歷史報章研究亦找到1952年的相似「老虎力量」意象， 以及石油公司在1959年及1960年使用的相同或相近版本。 因此，可以確定它源自廣告語言，但較難把唯一而確切的首次創作歸於某一個人。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "The advertisement originally promised greater engine power. Speakers later applied the same image to people. A person with “a tiger in their tank” was imagined as having an inner supply of power, enthusiasm and determination.",
            "zh": "這個廣告最初承諾為汽車引擎帶來更強動力。 後來，說話者把相同畫面應用於人物。 當一個人「油缸中有老虎」時，便象徵他內在充滿力量、熱誠及決心。"
          }
        ],
        "memoryEn": "Fuel powers a car; the imaginary tiger powers the person.",
        "memoryZh": "燃料為汽車提供動力；想像中的老虎則為人物注入幹勁。"
      },
