            "en": "Expressions based on ratios such as nine cases out of ten and nine times out of ten developed naturally as ways of describing probability and repeated experience. Jane Austen used the related wording “in nine cases out of ten” in Pride and Prejudice, published in 1813. The exact modern wording appears in William Cobbett’s Advice to Young Men, originally published in parts from 1829. These records show that this family of expressions was established in nineteenth-century English, but they do not prove who first created it.",
