        "history": [
          {
            "titleEn": "The Historical Background",
            "titleZh": "歷史背景",
            "en": "The adjective sick comes from Old English sēoc, meaning ill or unwell. Stomach entered Middle English through Old French and Latin, ultimately coming from a Greek word connected with the throat or digestive organ. These are the histories of the individual words, not proof of the exact idiom’s first use.",
            "zh": "形容詞 sick 源自古英語 sēoc，表示生病或身體不適。 Stomach 經古法語及拉丁語進入中古英語，最終源自一個與喉部或消化器官有關的希臘詞。 這些只是個別字詞的歷史，並不能證明完整表達首次出現的時間。"
          },
          {
            "titleEn": "How the Meaning Developed",
            "titleZh": "意思如何演變",
            "en": "The expression probably developed as a direct way of locating the feeling of sickness in the stomach. Over time, sick to one’s stomach became a conventional phrase for nausea or the feeling that vomiting may occur.",
            "zh": "這個表達很可能逐漸形成，用來直接指出不適感集中在胃部。 後來，sick to one’s stomach 成為表示反胃或可能嘔吐的固定說法。"
          }
        ],
